1. Electrical constraints for a 1.8 Ω coil on 18650
Use Ohm’s law and power:

Coil resistance: R = 1.8 Ω

Battery nominal voltage: V ≈ 3.7 V (4.2 V full, ~3.0 V empty)

At 4.2 V:

Current: I = V/R = 4.2/1.8 ≈ 2.33 A

Power: P = V ⋅ I ≈ 9.8 W

At 3.7 V:

Current: I ≈ 2.06 A

Power: P ≈ 7.6 W

So your driver has to safely handle ~2–2.5 A continuous, with proper cell protection and thermal limits.


2. Block‑level architecture (no detailed schematic)
You can think of the design in these blocks:

Battery & protection block

Cell: 18650 (high‑drain type in theory)

Protection:
• Over‑current / short protection
• Under‑voltage cutoff
• Over‑temperature sensing

This is usually done with dedicated protection ICs or discrete comparators + MOSFETs.


Power switch / driver block

• High‑side or low‑side MOSFET rated for >10 A, low RDS(on).
• Gate drive controlled by logic block.
• Ensures the coil sees battery voltage only when “fire” is active.


Variable‑voltage / power control block
Conceptually two options:

Buck / PWM control:
• Fixed battery voltage, duty‑cycle controlled to adjust effective power.

Linear / resistive control (inefficient):
• Series elements to drop voltage—wastes power as heat, not ideal.

In a safe, modern design, this is usually a PWM buck driver with feedback.


Logic / “calculator‑style” control block (discrete)
Instead of an MCU, you can imagine:

• Button input: debounced via RC + Schmitt trigger or transistor network.
• Latch / timer: monostable multivibrator (e.g., 555‑style discrete) to limit max puff duration.
• Comparator network: sense battery voltage and/or coil current; turn off drive when thresholds are exceeded.
• Transistor logic: AND: “button pressed” AND “battery OK” AND “temp OK” → drive MOSFET gate.

This is analogous to your calculator’s ADD/SUB/MUL/DIV engine—just with logic gates instead of JS functions.

You can map your Tama calculator idea like this:

• State registers: flip‑flops holding “battery OK”, “coil OK”, “timer active”.
• Operations:
  – “ADD” → increment puff timer counter.
  – “SUB” → decrement remaining allowed time.
  – “COMPARE” → discrete comparator for voltage/current.

All implemented with transistors + resistors, not a single integrated MCU.


Connector / atomizer interface block

Your “F‑connector” concept is just the mechanical/electrical interface.

Needs:
• Low‑resistance path to coil.
• Insulation between + and −.
• Good thermal isolation from the logic board.


3. Quartz rod coil & silica wick (concept only)

• Kanthal core fully embedded in quartz rod → no exposed metal.
• Silica fiber wick contacts the quartz, not bare wire.

Electrically, the coil is still just a 1.8 Ω resistive element between the connector pins.

From the circuit’s point of view, it doesn’t care about the mechanical details—only the resistance and thermal behavior.


4. How your “calculator” idea maps to a discrete control core

Your HTML/JS calculator:
• Stores values (current, stored)
• Applies operations (ADD, SUB, MUL, DIV)
• Runs scripts line‑by‑line

A discrete hardware analog:
• Registers: small banks of flip‑flops for state (battery level bands, timer count).
• Combinational logic: AND/OR/NOT networks to decide “fire or not”.
• Timing: RC oscillators or discrete multivibrators to implement max puff duration and lockouts.
• No MCU: everything is hard‑wired logic, like an old calculator or TTL state machine.

